   - [`06_outreach_volume_sizing.sql`](sql/03_analysis/06_outreach_volume_sizing.sql)
6. **Model and size** in Python: Kaplan-Meier, log-rank, Cox, Poisson rate ratios, sizing, pilot power: [`src/analysis.py`](src/analysis.py).
7. **Report:** figures, dashboard, Power BI export, and this README (rendered from `reports/results.json` by [`src/report.py`](src/report.py), so the numbers cannot drift from the pipeline).

Metric definitions are in [`docs/methodology.md`](docs/methodology.md).

## Key findings

### 1. Members with an early PCP visit stay longer, and the gap is largest for duals

Population: {{n_landmark}} members still enrolled at day 90 (the landmark). Exposure is measured only during the first 90 days; follow-up starts after it.

| Group | 12-month retention, PCP visit within 90 days | No visit | Gap (pp) |
|---|---|---|---|
| All | {{km_all_pcp1}} | {{km_all_pcp0}} | {{km_gap_all_pp}} |
| Non-dual | {{km_nondual_pcp1}} | {{km_nondual_pcp0}} | {{km_gap_nondual_pp}} |
| Dual-eligible | {{km_dual_pcp1}} | {{km_dual_pcp0}} | {{km_gap_dual_pp}} |

Log-rank chi-square = {{logrank_chi2}} (1 df). Cox hazard ratio for a PCP visit: **{{hr_unadj}}** unadjusted (95% CI {{hr_unadj_lo}}-{{hr_unadj_hi}}) and **{{hr_adj}}** adjusted (95% CI {{hr_adj_lo}}-{{hr_adj_hi}}), on {{cox_n}} members and {{cox_events}} disenrollments. Dual-eligible members visit a PCP less often and leave more often (HR {{hr_dual}}), so the raw comparison overstates the visit effect. Adjustment moves the estimate toward 1. The proportional-hazards check passes (Schoenfeld residual vs. time correlation {{ph_r}}, p = {{ph_p}}).

This is an association. Members who see a doctor early may be more engaged in ways the data does not capture. The sizing below treats the causal share as unknown.

![Hazard ratios vs planted values](figures/cox_hazard_ratios.png)

### 2. The onboarding funnel leaks at the HRA step for online enrollees

Welcome-call completion is flat across channels ({{welcome_all}} overall). The HRA step is where online members fall behind: {{hra_step_online}} of online members who completed the welcome call finished the HRA, against about {{hra_step_other}} for the other channels. Only {{hra_online}} of all online members complete an HRA in 90 days (all members: {{hra_all}}).

![Onboarding funnel by channel](figures/onboarding_funnel_by_channel.png)

### 3. Churn is seasonal, and snapshot reporting undercounts it

Disenrollment runs {{oep_rate}} per month in January-March vs {{non_oep_rate}} in the other months ({{oep_ratio}}x). Counting terminations by month-end snapshot gives {{snap_disenroll}} disenrollments in 2024-2025; counting by effective date gives {{eff_disenroll}}, so the snapshot view misses {{snapshot_undercount}}.

![Disenrollment seasonality](figures/disenrollment_seasonality.png)

### 4. Who to call first

Across {{seg_cells}} channel x plan type x dual cells, 12-month retention ranges from {{seg_worst_ret}} ({{seg_worst}}, n = {{seg_worst_n}}) to {{seg_best_ret}} ({{seg_best}}). Among members with no early PCP visit, dual-eligible broker enrollees lose the most: {{prio_churn}} leave within 12 months.

![Cohort retention heatmap](figures/cohort_retention_heatmap.png)

### 5. Sizing the outreach team

On average {{workload}} new members a month are still enrolled at day 60 with no PCP visit ({{nv_share60}} of members still enrolled at day 60). January cohorts peak at {{workload_peak}}. At {{attempts}} attempts of {{minutes}} minutes each and {{fte_hours}} productive hours per FTE-month, that is {{fte}} FTE on average ({{fte_peak}} in the January peak). Funding {{fte_funded}} FTE at a simulated {{fte_annual_cost}} each costs {{staff_cost}} a year.

If outreach raised the PCP-visit rate by 10 pp across one year of new members ({{new_2024}} enrollments in 2024), about {{extra_visitors}} more members would have an early visit. Using the adjusted hazard ratio, each converted member gains {{d_ret12}} pp of 12-month retention and {{d_mm}} member-months in months 4-12 ({{d_mm24}} through month 24, assuming the effect persists). At a simulated {{revenue_pmpm}} per member-month:

{{sizing_table}}

Break-even causal share: {{breakeven12}} on first-year revenue{{breakeven12_note}}, {{breakeven24}} on 24-month revenue.

A dual-only program needs {{dual_fte}} FTE ({{dual_fte_funded}} funded, {{dual_cost}}) for {{dual_workload}} members a month. It breaks even on 24-month revenue at a {{dual_breakeven24}} causal share. Rounding up to whole FTEs makes small programs less efficient per member.

